<p>President Donald Trump has discussed possible timelines for U.S. strikes on Iran in the coming days but has not made a final decision, according to multiple reports citing U.S. officials and unnamed sources. At the same time, the White House is urging Tehran to reach a nuclear deal, saying diplomacy is the wiser path than confrontation. Several outlets say the U.S. military is moving ships, aircraft, and other assets into position in the Middle East to increase pressure and keep strike options ready while talks continue. Iran, meanwhile, says it is still engaged in indirect talks — mediated by Oman — and U.S. officials expect a written Iranian proposal aimed at resolving the stand-off. The moment is high-stakes after a recent period of escalation cited in reporting, and amid renewed tensions following a deadly crackdown on protesters last month. Even with the threat of force hanging over the talks, the negotiating channel remains open — and could offer an off-ramp if both sides choose it.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/6w58QtErHMmbKS2xdeO0Nf70kE6FX8C6Im-BMQS7AyH6DpauOqI5KrFanpB8DAz1iQXZEM4Rqi641bXAAckkT8C8pevz1gmm1n5bhKK7JxUt' alt='White House press secretary Karoline Leavitt holds a briefing at the White House in Washington, DC, US on February 18, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Pressure messaging: In a Truth Social post, Trump warned Britain against giving up sovereignty over the Chagos Islands, arguing the Diego Garcia base could be needed if Iran does not reach a deal.</li><li>Force posture: The New York Times said the U.S. is repositioning forces as it weighs options, including moving an aircraft carrier toward the Middle East.</li><li>Recent context for the talks: Dawn reported that a previous negotiating push fell apart after Israel launched surprise strikes on Iran last June, sparking a 12-day war that the U.S. briefly joined when it bombed Iranian nuclear sites.</li><li>Deployment timing: A senior U.S. official told The Straits Times that advisers were told deployed forces should be in place by mid-March.</li></ul><blockquote>Iran would be very wise to make a deal with President Trump and with his administration - Karoline Leavitt</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>White House: Press Secretary Karoline Leavitt cast a negotiated deal as Iran’s best option, while the administration kept diplomacy on the table alongside military pressure. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1974344/white-house-tells-iran-to-do-deal-as-trump-hints-at-us-strikes'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>Iranian government: Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi said Iran is drafting a framework for future talks after Oman-mediated negotiations, signaling that Tehran wants to keep the diplomatic channel open. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1974344/white-house-tells-iran-to-do-deal-as-trump-hints-at-us-strikes'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>U.S. defense and security officials: Officials cited by U.S. media described a posture of readiness and a near-term strike window, while noting the president has not issued a final order. (<a href='https://www.cbsnews.com/news/trump-possible-timeline-iran-strikes/'>CBS News</a>)</li><li>Senior U.S. official: A U.S. official said Iran is expected to submit a written proposal, and that planned deployments were set to be in place by mid-March. (<a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/middle-east/iran-expected-to-make-written-proposal-for-resolving-standoff-with-us-senior-us-official-says'>The Straits Times</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.reddit.com/r/worldnews/comments/1r8i0mp/trump_has_discussed_timeline_for_iran_strikes/'>Trump has discussed timeline for Iran strikes — including as soon as this weekend — but no decision yet</a> - reddit.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/feb/18/trump-iran-us-military-reports'>US military ready for possible Iran strikes but Trump yet to make decision, reports say</a> - theguardian.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dawn.com/n